Samurai:
One of the hardest parts of starting a samurai is adjusting to the new
names given to certain objects.  "Ya" are arrows, "gunyoki" are food
rations, etc.; it can be frustrating to pick an object up and not know
what it actually is, but there aren't too many items that have been
renamed.  Beyond that, samurai are pretty much typical hack-and-slash
characters.  Samurai can #twoweapon, but don't try it until armor's not
as much of a concern.

The samurai quest is largely populated by wolves, ninja, and stalkers;
the ability to see invisible monsters will be helpful (and, if you
don't have it, it should be fairly easy to get by eating stalker
corpses).  The upper filler levels are swamp, while the middle level
and everything below it are castle.  There're some storerooms in the
middle level--there's a possibility that you'll find an artifact in the
weapons storeroom, so be sure to check.  The hardest part of the quest
is killing the quest nemesis before he kills you, as he will almost
definitely attack you with your quest reward; you really want to kill
him before he gets a chance to do a melee attack.  He's not resistant
to anything other than stoning, which helps.

The quest "reward" is the Tsurugi of Muramasa, which, quite frankly, is
best left in a locked chest somewhere.  When carried, it acts as a
luckstone; when wielded, it does extra damage to all monsters, plus has
a 5% chance of killing most monsters outright (large monsters just
suffer double damage).  This is good when you're the one doing the
wielding, but certain monsters will attempt to steal it from you, at
which point you could find yourself on the receiving end of the 5%
insta-kill.  It's also a two-handed weapon, which precludes using a
shield or #two-weaponing; if it gets cursed while wielded, you won't be
able to get into your bag.

Tourists:
It's possible that your version of Nethack may not offer tourists,
since the role is optional; however, since the default is to include
it, tourists will probably be available.  They can be a challenge to
play, especially in the early game, since they are better equipped for
Disney than the Dungeons.  Use any trick you can think of to survive;
throw those darts, hide behind your pet, and get a new weapon as soon
as possible--and take plenty of pictures, as the flash will blind
monsters, giving you a chance to escape.  Tourists will be overcharged
in shops, but they start with plenty of money.  Incidentally, none of
the shops will accept the credit card as payment, though it does have
other uses.  Tourists are very flexible; find your favorite play style
and enjoy.

The quest is largely populated by spiders, insects, and centaurs.
There are also a fair number of soldiers, many in barracks.  The filler
levels are caverns.  The Master of Thieves, the quest nemesis,
shouldn't be overly difficult--he has two regular attacks, and no magic
attacks.  Enjoy the scenery (and creative uses of monsters) in the
quest.

The quest reward is the Platinum Yendorian Express Card--an artifact
credit card that gives magic resistance, telepathy, and half spell
damage when carried.  As an added (and very useful) bonus, you can
charge items with it by #invoking, so you don't need to scrounge for
charging scrolls.  This is subject to the standard #invoke timeouts,
however, so you can't do it too frequently.
